$ cat -n '/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-trade/2da9385f-4fe4-506a-be3d-78153c549662/scratchpad/cat8/venvs/8-026/openclaw/docs/concepts/mantis.md'
     1	---
     2	summary: "Mantis captures visual end-to-end evidence for live transport comparisons and focused candidate-only browser proofs, then attaches the artifacts to PRs."
     3	title: "Mantis"
     4	read_when:
     5	  - Building or running live visual QA for OpenClaw bugs
     6	  - Adding before and after verification for a pull request
     7	  - Adding Discord, Slack, WhatsApp, or other live transport scenarios
     8	  - Running focused Control UI browser proof for a candidate ref
     9	  - Debugging QA runs that need screenshots, browser automation, or VNC access
    10	---
    11	
    12	Mantis publishes visual CI evidence and a PR comment for OpenClaw behavior.
    13	Live transport scenarios compare a known-bad baseline with a candidate ref;
    14	focused browser lanes may instead prove one candidate against a deterministic
    15	mocked transport. Discord shipped first with real bot auth, guild channels, reactions, threads,
    16	and a browser witness. Slack and focused Control UI chat lanes exist too;
    17	WhatsApp and Matrix are unimplemented.
    18	
    19	## Ownership
    20	
    21	- OpenClaw (`extensions/qa-lab/src/mantis/*`): scenario runtime, `pnpm openclaw qa mantis <command>` CLI, evidence schema.
    22	- QA Lab (`extensions/qa-lab/src/live-transports/*`): live transport harness, driver/SUT bots, report/evidence writers.
    23	- Crabbox (`openclaw/crabbox`): warmed Linux machines, leases, VNC, `crabbox media preview`.
    24	- GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/mantis-*.yml`): remote entrypoints, artifact retention.
    25	- ClawSweeper: independently reviews proof and owns review/readiness policy. Mantis workflow dispatch and evidence publication are separate from ordinary review publication; a Mantis result does not itself grant readiness or merge permission.
    26	
    27	## CLI commands
    28	
    29	All commands are `pnpm openclaw qa mantis <command>`, defined in
    30	`extensions/qa-lab/src/mantis/cli.ts`. Requires `OPENCLAW_ENABLE_PRIVATE_QA_CLI=1`
    31	at build/run time (bundled workflows set `OPENCLAW_BUILD_PRIVATE_QA=1` and
    32	`OPENCLAW_ENABLE_PRIVATE_QA_CLI=1` before building).
    33	
    34	| Command                         | Purpose                                                                                                                                                   |
    35	| ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
    36	| `discord-smoke`                 | Verify the Mantis Discord bot can see the guild/channel, post, and react.                                                                                 |
    37	| `run`                           | Run a before/after scenario against baseline and candidate refs (Discord only).                                                                           |
    38	| `desktop-browser-smoke`         | Lease/reuse a Crabbox desktop, open a visible browser, capture screenshot + video.                                                                        |
    39	| `slack-desktop-smoke`           | Lease/reuse a Crabbox desktop, run Slack QA inside it, open Slack Web, capture evidence.                                                                  |
    40	| `visual-task` / `visual-driver` | Generic Crabbox desktop capture with optional image-understanding assertions; `visual-driver` is the driver half launched under `crabbox record --while`. |
    41	
    42	Every command accepts `--repo-root <path>` and `--output-dir <path>`; Crabbox
    43	commands also accept `--crabbox-bin`, `--provider`, `--machine-class`/`--class`,
    44	`--lease-id`, `--idle-timeout`, `--ttl`, and `--keep-lease`. Local CLI defaults
    45	for provider/class are `hetzner`/`beast` unless noted otherwise; CI workflows
    46	usually override both.
    47	
    48	### `discord-smoke`
    49	
    50	```bash
    51	pnpm openclaw qa mantis discord-smoke \
    52	  --output-dir .artifacts/qa-e2e/mantis/discord-smoke
    53	```
    54	
    55	Calls the Discord REST API (`https://discord.com/api/v10`) to fetch the bot
    56	user, the guild, the guild's channels, and the target channel, asserts the
    57	channel belongs to the guild, then (unless `--skip-post`) posts a message and
    58	adds a `👀` reaction. Writes `mantis-discord-smoke-summary.json` and
    59	`mantis-discord-smoke-report.md`.
    60	
    61	Token resolution order: `--token-file` value, then `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_MANTIS_BOT_TOKEN`
    62	(override with `--token-env`), then a file named by `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_MANTIS_BOT_TOKEN_FILE`
    63	(override with `--token-file-env`). Guild/channel ids come from
    64	`OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_GUILD_ID` / `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_CHANNEL_ID` (override with
    65	`--guild-id` / `--channel-id`) and must be 17-20 digit Discord snowflakes. Set
    66	`OPENCLAW_QA_REDACT_PUBLIC_METADATA=1` to replace bot/guild/channel/message ids
    67	and names with `<redacted>` in the published summary and report.
    68	
    69	### `run`
    70	
    71	```bash
    72	pnpm openclaw qa mantis run \
    73	  --transport discord \
    74	  --scenario discord-status-reactions-tool-only \
    75	  --baseline origin/main \
    76	  --candidate HEAD \
    77	  --output-dir .artifacts/qa-e2e/mantis/local-discord-status-reactions
    78	```
    79	
    80	`--transport` currently only accepts `discord`. `--scenario` is one of two
    81	built-in ids, each with its own default baseline ref and expected before/after
    82	labels (`extensions/qa-lab/src/mantis/run.runtime.ts`):
    83	
    84	| Scenario                                   | Default baseline                           | Baseline expects                         | Candidate expects            |
    85	| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
    86	| `discord-status-reactions-tool-only`       | `0bf06e953fdda290799fc9fb9244a8f67fdae593` | `queued-only`                            | `queued -> thinking -> done` |
    87	| `discord-thread-reply-filepath-attachment` | `81349cdc2a9d5143fd0991ed858b739e7d96e05c` | thread reply omits `filePath` attachment | thread reply includes it     |
    88	
    89	`--candidate` defaults to `HEAD`. Other flags: `--credential-source`
    90	(default `convex`), `--credential-role` (default `ci`), `--provider-mode`
    91	(default `live-frontier`), `--fast` (default on), `--skip-install`, `--skip-build`.
    92	
    93	The runner treats `--output-dir` as a stable container. It must name a relative
    94	child directory inside the repository; the repository root (`.`), absolute
    95	paths, and paths that escape the repository are rejected. Detached `git
    96	worktree` checkouts live under `<output-dir>.worktrees/`. Each checkout has a
    97	unique `<lane>-<run-id>` name, so an interrupted run cannot collide with a
    98	later baseline or candidate. The runner runs `pnpm install`/`pnpm build` in
    99	each checkout (unless skipped), then runs
   100	`pnpm openclaw qa discord --scenario <id> --model openai/gpt-5.4 --alt-model openai/gpt-5.4 --allow-failures`
   101	against each worktree. Each lane writes `discord-qa-reaction-timelines.json`
   102	plus a `<scenario-id>-timeline.html`/`.png` pair. Before asking Git to remove
   103	the checkout, the runner copies the lane evidence into a private staging
   104	directory under `<output-dir>`.
   105	
   106	After both lanes and their cleanup finish, the runner adds `comparison.json`,
   107	`mantis-report.md`, and `mantis-evidence.json` to the staged set, then replaces
   108	the five stable entries together with rollback on publication failure. A failed
   109	attempt preserves the preceding complete evidence set and writes `error.txt`;
   110	a successful attempt removes an older `error.txt`. Existing automation
   111	continues reading the stable paths directly. Use a distinct output directory
   112	for each concurrently running command; publication to one shared output
   113	directory is not serialized. Existing unrelated top-level files are preserved.
   114	The command exits nonzero if the comparison did not pass (baseline `fail` and
   115	candidate `pass`).
   116	
   117	The second Discord scenario (`discord-thread-reply-filepath-attachment`) posts
   118	a parent message with the driver bot, creates a real thread, calls the SUT's
   119	`message.thread-reply` action with a repo-local `filePath`, then polls the
   120	thread for the reply and the attachment filename. It expects an attachment
   121	named `mantis-thread-report.md`.
   122	
   123	### `desktop-browser-smoke`
   124	
   125	```bash
   126	pnpm openclaw qa mantis desktop-browser-smoke \
   127	  --output-dir .artifacts/qa-e2e/mantis/desktop-browser
   128	```
   129	
   130	Leases or reuses a Crabbox desktop, launches a browser inside the VNC session
   131	pointed at `--browser-url` (default `https://openclaw.ai`) or a rendered
   132	`--html-file`, waits, screenshots with `scrot`, optionally records an MP4 with
   133	`ffmpeg`, and rsyncs `desktop-browser-smoke.png` / `.mp4` / `remote-metadata.json`
   134	back to `--output-dir`.
   135	
   136	Flags:
   137	
   138	- `--lease-id <cbx_...>` reuses a warmed desktop instead of creating one.
   139	- `--browser-profile-dir <remote-path>` reuses a remote Chrome user-data-dir so a persistent desktop stays logged in between runs (used for a long-lived Discord Web viewer profile).
   140	- `--browser-profile-archive-env <name>` restores a base64 `.tgz` Chrome profile archive from that env var before launch (default `OPENCLAW_MANTIS_BROWSER_PROFILE_TGZ_B64`); used for logged-in witnesses like Discord Web.
   141	- `--video-duration <seconds>` controls MP4 capture length (default 10s).
   142	- `--keep-lease` (or `OPENCLAW_MANTIS_KEEP_VM=1`) keeps a lease this run created open for VNC inspection; failed runs that created a lease also keep it by default.
   143	
   144	For Discord Web evidence, Mantis uses a dedicated viewer account, not a bot
   145	token. The Discord REST oracle (via `qa discord`) remains authoritative; when
   146	`OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_CAPTURE_UI_METADATA=1` is set, the scenario also writes a
   147	Discord Web URL artifact, and `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_KEEP_THREADS=1` leaves the
   148	thread open long enough for the browser to open it.
   149	
   150	The GitHub workflow prefers a persistent viewer profile via
   151	`MANTIS_DISCORD_VIEWER_CHROME_PROFILE_DIR` (full profile archives can outgrow
   152	GitHub's secret size limit); for small/bootstrap profiles it can restore a
   153	base64 `.tgz` from `MANTIS_DISCORD_VIEWER_CHROME_PROFILE_TGZ_B64` instead. With
   154	neither source configured, the workflow still publishes the deterministic
   155	baseline/candidate screenshots and logs that the logged-in witness was
   156	skipped.
   157	
   158	### `slack-desktop-smoke`
   159	
   160	```bash
   161	pnpm openclaw qa mantis slack-desktop-smoke \
   162	  --output-dir .artifacts/qa-e2e/mantis/slack-desktop \
   163	  --gateway-setup \
   164	  --scenario slack-canary \
   165	  --keep-lease
   166	```
   167	
   168	Leases or reuses a Crabbox desktop, syncs the checkout into the VM, runs
   169	`pnpm openclaw qa slack` inside it, opens Slack Web in the VNC browser,
   170	captures the desktop, and copies both the Slack QA artifacts (`slack-qa/`) and
   171	the VNC screenshot/video back locally. This is the only Mantis shape where the
   172	SUT gateway and the browser both run inside the same VM.
   173	
   174	With `--gateway-setup`, the command creates a persistent disposable OpenClaw
   175	home at `$HOME/.openclaw-mantis/slack-openclaw` in the VM, patches Slack
   176	Socket Mode config for the target channel, starts
   177	`openclaw gateway run --dev --allow-unconfigured --port 38973`, and leaves
   178	Chrome running in the VNC session; omitting `--gateway-setup` runs the normal
   179	bot-to-bot Slack QA lane instead.
   180	
   181	Required env for `--credential-source env` (local default is `env`; role
   182	default is `maintainer`):
   183	
   184	- `OPENCLAW_QA_SLACK_CHANNEL_ID`
   185	- `OPENCLAW_QA_SLACK_DRIVER_BOT_TOKEN`
   186	- `OPENCLAW_QA_SLACK_SUT_BOT_TOKEN`
   187	- `OPENCLAW_QA_SLACK_SUT_APP_TOKEN`
   188	- `OPENCLAW_LIVE_OPENAI_KEY` for the remote model lane (if only `OPENAI_API_KEY`
   189	  is set locally, Mantis copies it to `OPENCLAW_LIVE_OPENAI_KEY` before
   190	  invoking Crabbox)
   191	
   192	With `--credential-source convex`, Mantis leases the Slack SUT credential from
   193	the shared pool before creating the VM and forwards channel id, app token, and
   194	bot token into the VM as `OPENCLAW_MANTIS_SLACK_*` env vars, so GitHub
   195	workflows only need the Convex broker secret, not raw Slack tokens.
   196	
   197	Other flags: `--slack-url <url>` opens a specific URL (otherwise Mantis derives
   198	`https://app.slack.com/client/<team>/<channel>` from `auth.test`);
   199	`--slack-channel-id <id>` sets the gateway allowlist channel;
   200	`OPENCLAW_MANTIS_SLACK_BROWSER_PROFILE_DIR` controls the persistent Chrome
   201	profile inside the VM (default `$HOME/.config/openclaw-mantis/slack-chrome-profile`);
   202	`--approval-checkpoints` runs the native Slack approval scenarios
   203	(`slack-approval-exec-native`, `slack-approval-plugin-native`) and renders
   204	pending/resolved checkpoint screenshots instead of gateway setup (mutually
   205	exclusive with `--gateway-setup`); `--hydrate-mode source|prehydrated`,
   206	`--provider-mode`, `--model`, `--alt-model`, and `--fast` pass through to the
   207	Slack live lane.
   208	
   209	Approval checkpoint screenshots are rendered from the Slack API message the
   210	scenario observed, not the live Slack UI; `slack-desktop-smoke.png` is only
   211	proof of Slack Web itself when the lease's browser profile was already logged
   212	in.
   213	
   214	## Evidence manifest
   215	
   216	The publisher requires schema version 2 of `mantis-evidence.json` next to
   217	the report. Each included lane must declare `expectationMet`; the publisher
   218	downgrades a claimed pass when a lane's expectation was not met. For example:
   219	
   220	```json
   221	{
   222	  "schemaVersion": 2,
   223	  "id": "discord-status-reactions",
   224	  "title": "Mantis Discord Status Reactions QA",
   225	  "summary": "Human-readable top summary for the PR comment.",
   226	  "scenario": "discord-status-reactions-tool-only",
   227	  "comparison": {
   228	    "baseline": {
   229	      "sha": "<baseline-sha>",
   230	      "status": "fail",
   231	      "expected": "queued-only",
   232	      "expectationMet": true
   233	    },
   234	    "candidate": {
   235	      "sha": "<candidate-sha>",
   236	      "status": "pass",
   237	      "expected": "queued -> thinking -> done",
   238	      "expectationMet": true
   239	    },
   240	    "pass": true,
   241	    "outcome": "pass"
   242	  },
   243	  "artifacts": [
   244	    {
   245	      "kind": "timeline",
   246	      "lane": "baseline",
   247	      "label": "Baseline queued-only",
   248	      "path": "baseline/timeline.png",
   249	      "targetPath": "baseline.png",
   250	      "alt": "Baseline Discord timeline",
   251	      "width": 420
   252	    }
   253	  ]
   254	}
   255	```
   256	
   257	This manifest is a presentation contract, not an authenticated proof receipt.
   258	It does not independently establish repository/PR ownership, current-head
   259	freshness, execution authority, or the truth of an assertion. A video, process
   260	exit code, or declared expectation alone is not sufficient behavioral proof.
   261	Review the underlying observations and execution provenance before relying on
   262	the result. Infrastructure failures and missing observations are inconclusive,
   263	not evidence that the baseline reproduced the bug.
   264	
   265	The local `qa mantis run` producer still emits schema version 1, which the
   266	publisher rejects. Do not relabel that output as version 2 without deriving
   267	its lane expectations from observations; the workflow producers emit version 2.
   268	
   269	Artifact `path` is relative to the manifest's directory; `targetPath` is
   270	relative to the configured R2/S3 artifact prefix. `scripts/mantis/publish-pr-evidence.mjs`
   271	rejects path traversal and skips entries with `"required": false` when the
   272	file is missing.
   273	
   274	Artifact kinds: `timeline` (deterministic before/after screenshot),
   275	`desktopScreenshot` (VNC/browser screenshot), `motionPreview` (inline animated
   276	GIF from the recording), `motionClip` (motion-trimmed MP4), `fullVideo` (full
   277	recording), `metadata` (JSON/log sidecar), `report` (Markdown report).
   278	
   279	For `qa mantis run`, the stable output container has this layout:
   280	
   281	```text
   282	.artifacts/qa-e2e/mantis/<run-id>/
   283	  error.txt # failures only
   284	  mantis-report.md
   285	  mantis-evidence.json
   286	  baseline/
   287	  candidate/
   288	  comparison.json
   289	.artifacts/qa-e2e/mantis/<run-id>.worktrees/
   290	  baseline-<pid>-<uuid>/
   291	  candidate-<pid>-<uuid>/
   292	```
   293	
   294	The worktree root is normally empty after a completed run. Lane directories
   295	appear while active or when cleanup deliberately preserves one for diagnosis.
   296	On interruption, command settlement and artifact staging consume the same
   297	cleanup budget as Git removal. If that budget expires, or command descendants
   298	cannot be confirmed stopped, Mantis retains the worktree for inspection.
   299	The default local command runner requires Linux or macOS process-tree ownership.
   300	It refuses Windows stages before spawning a command because descendant
   301	extinction cannot currently be verified there; any prepared directory is retained.
   302	If `git worktree add` fails before registration, Mantis can leave its empty,
   303	uniquely named prepared directory instead of deleting through a pathname that
   304	could have been replaced.
   305	
   306	Each failed attempt writes `<output-dir>/error.txt`. The thrown error, or the
   307	concise stderr diagnostic for an expected CLI interrupt, reports that exact
   308	path. Mantis does not recursively delete a worktree after Git no longer owns
   309	its registration: if cleanup fails, inspect the retained unique directory
   310	under `<output-dir>.worktrees/` together with the reported `error.txt`, then
   311	remove it through Git after resolving the failure. If an owned path disappears
   312	while its Git registration remains, cleanup fails closed instead of recreating
   313	the path. At startup, Mantis also checks the exact historical
   314	`<output-dir>/worktrees/baseline` and `<output-dir>/worktrees/candidate` paths
   315	and removes them through Git when they are still registered and present. Other
   316	registered or unregistered entries in that legacy directory are left
   317	untouched.
   318	
   319	Screenshots are evidence, not secrets, but still need redaction discipline:
   320	private channel names, usernames, or message content may appear. Set
   321	`OPENCLAW_QA_REDACT_PUBLIC_METADATA=1` for public artifact uploads; it is
   322	enabled by default in the Discord and Slack GitHub workflows.
   323	
   324	## GitHub automation
   325	
   326	`scripts/mantis/publish-pr-evidence.mjs` is the reusable publisher. Workflows
   327	call it with the manifest, target PR, artifact target root, comment marker,
   328	artifact URL, run URL, and request source. It uploads declared artifacts to
   329	the Mantis R2 bucket, builds a summary-first PR comment with inline
   330	images/previews and linked videos, then updates the existing marker comment or
   331	creates a new one. Required env:
   332	
   333	- `MANTIS_ARTIFACT_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID`
   334	- `MANTIS_ARTIFACT_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`
   335	- `MANTIS_ARTIFACT_R2_BUCKET` (workflows set `openclaw-crabbox-artifacts`)
   336	- `MANTIS_ARTIFACT_R2_ENDPOINT`
   337	- `MANTIS_ARTIFACT_R2_REGION` (workflows set `auto`)
   338	- `MANTIS_ARTIFACT_R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` (workflows set `https://artifacts.openclaw.ai`)
   339	
   340	Comments post through the Mantis GitHub App (`MANTIS_GITHUB_APP_ID` /
   341	`MANTIS_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY`), not `github-actions[bot]`, using a hidden
   342	marker comment as the upsert key.
   343	
   344	| Workflow                          | Trigger         | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
   345	| --------------------------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   346	| `Mantis Discord Smoke`            | manual dispatch | Runs `discord-smoke` against a chosen ref.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |
   347	| `Mantis Discord Status Reactions` | manual dispatch | Builds separate baseline/candidate worktrees, runs `discord-status-reactions-tool-only` on each, renders each lane's timeline in a Crabbox desktop browser, generates motion-trimmed GIF/MP4 previews with `crabbox media preview`, uploads artifacts, posts inline PR evidence.       |
   348	| `Mantis Scenario`                 | manual dispatch | Generic dispatcher: takes `scenario_id` (`discord-status-reactions-tool-only`, `discord-thread-reply-filepath-attachment`, `slack-desktop-smoke`, `web-ui-chat-proof`), `baseline_ref`, `candidate_ref`, `pr_number`, and forwards to the matching scenario workflow.                  |
   349	| `Mantis Slack Desktop Smoke`      | manual dispatch | Leases a Crabbox Linux desktop (defaults to `aws`, choice of `hetzner`), runs `slack-desktop-smoke --gateway-setup` against the candidate, records the desktop, generates a motion preview, uploads artifacts, posts PR evidence when a PR number is given.                            |
   350	| `Mantis Web UI Chat Proof`        | manual dispatch | Runs the focused OpenClaw Control UI chat Playwright proof against the candidate, verifies the browser sends through the mocked Gateway, captures screenshot/video artifacts, and posts PR evidence. This lane is web chat proof only, not WinUI/native-app or arbitrary visual proof. |
   351	
   352	`Mantis Discord Status Reactions` accepts `baseline_ref`/`candidate_ref` and
   353	validates that the resolved SHA is either an
   354	ancestor of `origin/main`, a release tag (`v*`), or the head of an open PR
   355	before running with secret-bearing credentials.
   356	
   357	The legacy scenario workflows above remain available through manual Actions dispatch.
   358	
   359	Do not rely on the former `@clawsweeper mantis ...` example as a dedicated
   360	dispatch command. ClawSweeper's current command parser routes an unrecognized
   361	mention to general assistance, not a typed Mantis dispatch.
   362	
   363	The separate request-bound integration in
   364	[ClawSweeper #1425](https://github.com/openclaw/clawsweeper/pull/1425) and
   365	[OpenClaw #138953](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/138953) lets the
   366	reviewer select relevant proof before completing its original review. It does
   367	not restore automatic post-review recording or require every check on every PR.
   368	Hosted execution requires the trusted producer workflows on `main` and the
   369	matching ClawSweeper Worker/runtime deployment; merging documentation does not
   370	activate it. The existing Convex credential service is still required, but its
   371	APIs are reused without a Convex schema change or deployment.
   372	
   373	Once that integration is deployed, a human maintainer can request a review with
   374	proof available, without supplying a SHA:
   375	
   376	```text
   377	@clawsweeper proof
   378	@clawsweeper proof web-ui-chat-proof
   379	@clawsweeper proof telegram-bot-e2e-proof
   380	@clawsweeper proof web-ui-chat-proof,telegram-bot-e2e-proof
   381	```
   382	
   383	The bare command lets the reviewer select useful checks; explicit selections
   384	request each named check. The review resolves the current PR head. Control UI
   385	supports the fixed chat smoke against a mocked Gateway; Telegram supports a
   386	bounded, data-only Test Server plan. The legacy Crabline recipe is not a third
   387	inline tool. Missing, late, or incomplete observations remain inconclusive.
   388	The reviewer assesses results in the same review; successful execution alone
   389	does not clear proof, other readiness blockers, or merge requirements.
   390	
   391	Selected checks share a 20-minute proof ceiling, further bounded by the original
   392	review's remaining time minus its final-decision reserve. With the default
   393	20-minute review timeout and 90-second reserve, that is at most 18m30s if invoked
   394	immediately, less after analysis. This is a ceiling, not a fixed wait or a fresh
   395	budget per check. A consumer timeout does not itself cancel a dispatched producer;
   396	producer cleanup and credential/lease limits remain separate.
   397	
   398	### Telegram proof is a separate QA entrypoint
   399	
   400	Telegram is not an option in `mantis-scenario.yml` or `qa mantis run`.
   401	`pnpm openclaw qa telegram` uses the QA Lab Telegram adapter and the repository
   402	skill at `.agents/skills/telegram-e2e-userbot/SKILL.md`. The skill also supports
   403	focused real-user recordings through its own runner.
   404	
   405	The skill uses TDLib against Telegram's Test Server, with an exclusively leased
   406	Convex QA credential, a fresh Gateway, and an independent user observer.
   407	Prerequisites include a dependency-ready exact-ref runtime, the pinned TDLib
   408	setup, authenticated broker access, and distinct unused Gateway/provider ports.
   409	Its doctor acquires a lease and contacts Telegram: it is a live operation, not
   410	an offline readiness check. Do not run it without authorization for test-account
   411	activity.
   412	
   413	The recorder can observe messages, edits, deletions, reactions, and typing.
   414	Judge events after the recorded stimulus from the selected SUT, correlate
   415	message IDs and expected provider requests, and verify cleanup. The QA adapter's
   416	current driver exposes a narrower message/edit stream; choose the entrypoint
   417	whose observations actually cover the claim. A generic successful reply does
   418	not prove formatting, reaction, lifecycle, or threading behavior.
   419	
   420	Read the skill and its feature verification map before a Telegram exercise.
   421	The credential lease is not a scenario sandbox: custom command actions can use
   422	the leased test identity. Keep credential handling and execution in an explicitly
   423	authorized, isolated worker, never an ordinary read-only review.
   424	
   425	### Selected proof inside a ClawSweeper review
   426	
   427	The request-bound proof integration lets ClawSweeper choose a relevant check
   428	before finishing its current review. It does not run every check on every PR.
   429	The exact PR head is resolved by the review, not typed by the maintainer.
   430	`@clawsweeper proof` requests one review with proof available as a manual override;
   431	the result is evaluated in that review rather than generating a second review.
   432	
   433	The automatic surfaces are:
   434	
   435	- Telegram Test Server: a bounded, data-only plan of tester messages and button
   436	  clicks, deterministic model replies, streaming/native-command settings, and
   437	  the behavior the reviewer needs to observe. This can cover more than a generic
   438	  greeting, including selected formatting or command behavior.
   439	- Control UI: the existing fixed chat smoke recipe against a mocked Gateway.
   440	  This is not an arbitrary browser-task runner or proof of all UI behavior.
   441	
   442	The separate fixed Crabline recipe remains a producer entrypoint; it is not a
   443	third automatic tool or a mandatory three-check batch.
   444	
   445	A trusted external controller owns the Telegram userbot and real bot token.
   446	The candidate receives a disposable token alias and a restricted DM API proxy,
   447	not the QA lease, Telegram session, GitHub token, or deployment credentials.
   448	The candidate runs in a disposable Crabbox local-container on an internal
   449	network without a Docker socket. Deployment requires a Linux/Podman environment
   450	where this isolated Crabbox SSH lifecycle has been verified; a generic Docker
   451	smoke run does not establish that compatibility.
   452	
   453	The controller reuses the existing Convex acquire, heartbeat, payload, and
   454	release APIs. No Convex schema update, quarantine endpoint, or broker deployment
   455	is required. Review ownership and one-run authorization are checked by the
   456	ClawSweeper service using the trusted workflow's GitHub Actions OIDC identity.
   457	Expired or revoked authority stops privileged sends and proxy forwarding.
   458	A recorder parent-death guard stops the recorder if its controller exits.
   459	These safeguards do not claim that native TDLib background traffic is zero.
   460	
   461	The Telegram artifact contains the bounded complete timeline, provider requests,
   462	formatting entities, and button labels with known private values redacted.
   463	Oversized or incomplete observations fail closed. Its assertion outcome stays
   464	`inconclusive`: the original reviewer must assess the observations against the
   465	claim. A green process exit, canned reply, or video does not automatically clear
   466	proof or other readiness blockers. Bot registration/webhook operations are
   467	simulated; live production Telegram, groups, media, and unrestricted agent
   468	commands are outside this bounded plan.
   469	Transport profile display names are synthetic, while routing IDs and the selected
   470	bot username are retained where required. Message text is not rewritten by this
   471	projection. Tests of real profile names or username-based tester routing are
   472	outside this proof surface.
   473	
   474	## Machines and secrets
   475	
   476	Local CLI Crabbox defaults are `--provider hetzner --class beast`; override
   477	with `--provider`, `--class`/`--machine-class`, or
   478	`OPENCLAW_MANTIS_CRABBOX_PROVIDER` / `OPENCLAW_MANTIS_CRABBOX_CLASS`. GitHub
   479	workflows commonly override both (for example `--class standard`, and the
   480	Slack workflow's `aws`/`hetzner` provider choice input). If a provider is too
   481	slow or unavailable, add it behind the same Crabbox interface rather than
   482	hardcoding a fallback.
   483	
   484	VM baseline: Linux with a desktop-capable Chrome/Chromium, CDP access, VNC/
   485	noVNC, Node 24.16+ or 26.1+ and pnpm, an OpenClaw checkout, and
   486	outbound access to the target transport, GitHub, model providers, and the
   487	credential broker.
   488	
   489	Credential and environment names used across Mantis commands and workflows:
   490	
   491	- `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_MANTIS_BOT_TOKEN`
   492	- `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_GUILD_ID`
   493	- `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_CHANNEL_ID`
   494	- Local `qa mantis run --credential-source env` also requires
   495	  `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_DRIVER_BOT_TOKEN`, `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_SUT_BOT_TOKEN`,
   496	  and `OPENCLAW_QA_DISCORD_SUT_APPLICATION_ID`. GitHub workflows normally use
   497	  `--credential-source convex` and the broker credentials below instead of raw
   498	  Discord bot tokens.
   499	- `OPENCLAW_QA_REDACT_PUBLIC_METADATA=1` for public artifact uploads
   500	- `OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SITE_URL`, `OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SECRET_CI`
   501	- `OPENAI_API_KEY`
   502	- `CRABBOX_COORDINATOR` / `CRABBOX_COORDINATOR_TOKEN` (workflows also accept
   503	  `OPENCLAW_QA_MANTIS_CRABBOX_COORDINATOR` / `_TOKEN` as a fallback and map
   504	  them onto the plain names before invoking Crabbox)
   505	- `CRABBOX_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID`, `CRABBOX_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET`
   506	- `MANTIS_GITHUB_APP_ID`, `MANTIS_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY`
   507	
   508	The Mantis runner must never print Discord or Slack bot tokens,
   509	provider API keys, browser cookies, auth profile contents, VNC passwords, or
   510	raw credential payloads. If a token leaks into an issue, PR, chat, or log,
   511	rotate it after the replacement secret is stored.
   512	
   513	## Run outcomes
   514	
   515	Before/after transport scenarios distinguish these outcomes so a flaky
   516	environment does not read as a product regression:
   517	
   518	- **Bug reproduced**: baseline failed the way the scenario expects.
   519	- **Harness failure**: environment setup, credentials, transport API, browser,
   520	  or provider failed before the oracle was meaningful.
   521	
   522	Candidate-only browser proof reports whether the candidate passed the mocked
   523	Gateway and visible UI assertions; it does not claim baseline reproduction.
   524	
   525	## Adding a scenario
   526	
   527	Live transport scenarios are TypeScript-defined per transport (see
   528	`MANTIS_SCENARIO_CONFIGS` in `extensions/qa-lab/src/mantis/run.runtime.ts` for
   529	the Discord before/after shape), not a standalone declarative file format.
   530	Each scenario needs: id and title, transport, required credentials, baseline
   531	ref policy, candidate ref policy, OpenClaw config patch, setup/stimulus steps,
   532	expected baseline and candidate oracle, visual capture targets, timeout
   533	budget, and cleanup steps.
   534	
   535	Focused candidate-only browser proof can use a dedicated deterministic E2E test
   536	and workflow. Keep its scope explicit, validate the candidate ref before
   537	execution, isolate secret-backed publishing, and emit the same evidence
   538	manifest contract.
   539	
   540	Prefer small, typed oracles over vision checks: Discord reaction state or
   541	message references, Slack thread `ts`/reaction API state, email message ids
   542	and headers. Use browser screenshots when UI is the only reliable observable,
   543	and keep vision checks additive to a platform-API oracle where one exists.
   544	
   545	After Discord and Slack, the same runner shape extends to WhatsApp (QR login,
   546	re-identification, delivery, media, reactions) and Matrix (encrypted rooms,
   547	thread/reply relations, restart resume); neither is implemented yet.
   548	
   549	## Open questions
   550	
   551	- Which Discord bot should be the driver vs. the SUT when the existing Mantis
   552	  bot is reused?
   553	- How long should GitHub retain Mantis artifacts for PRs?
   554	- Should screenshots be redacted or cropped before upload for public PRs?
