     1	---
     2	summary: "Outbound message lifecycle API for channel plugins: adapters, receipts, durable sends, live preview, and reply pipeline helpers"
     3	title: "Channel outbound API"
     4	doc-schema-version: 1
     5	read_when:
     6	  - You are building or refactoring a messaging channel plugin send path
     7	  - You need durable final reply delivery, receipts, live preview finalization, or receive acknowledgement policy
     8	  - You are migrating from channel-message or legacy reply dispatch helpers
     9	---
    10	
    11	Channel plugins expose outbound message behavior from
    12	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-outbound`. Use
    13	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-inbound` for receive/context/dispatch
    14	orchestration.
    15	
    16	Core owns queueing, durability, the durable **ingress monitor and drain**
    17	(`createChannelIngressMonitor`, `createChannelIngressDrain`, and
    18	`openChannelIngressDrain`), generic retry policy, turn-adoption lifecycle
    19	(`turnAdoptionLifecycle` / `bindIngressLifecycleToReplyOptions`), hooks,
    20	receipts, and the shared `message` tool. The plugin owns native
    21	send/edit/delete calls, target normalization, platform threading, selected
    22	quotes, notification flags, account state, ingress inspection and payload
    23	encoding, lane keys, non-retryable predicates, optional supersede
    24	authorization, and platform-specific side effects.
    25	
    26	## Durable ingress monitors
    27	
    28	Use `createChannelIngressMonitor(...)` when a channel must persist accepted
    29	transport events before dispatch. It composes a channel ingress queue and drain
    30	with the shared admission, polling, pruning, delivery, and shutdown lifecycle.
    31	Use the lower-level `createChannelIngressDrain(...)` only when the transport
    32	owns a materially different admission or pump contract.
    33	
    34	The required options are:
    35	
    36	| Option                           | Contract                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
    37	| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
    38	| `queue`                          | A `ChannelIngressQueue`, or a lazy factory that opens the account-scoped queue.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |
    39	| `inspect(raw, context)`          | Returns the stable `eventId` and serialized `laneKey`, or `null` for an ignored event. Claim-time facts must match the persisted id and lane.                                                                                                                                                                    |
    40	| `payload`                        | Supplies the payload version plus body serialization/deserialization. Use `storage: "raw-event"` for the standard `{ version, rawEvent }` string envelope, or provide custom encode/decode callbacks for an existing channel-specific shape. `createClaimError` classifies invalid versions or changed identity. |
    41	| `deliver(raw, lifecycle, claim)` | Dispatches one decoded event and receives the complete adoption lifecycle. It may return `completed`, `deferred`, `failed-retryable`, or nothing.                                                                                                                                                                |
    42	| `pollIntervalMs`                 | Schedules recovery/drain polls while the monitor is running.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
    43	| `retention`                      | Supplies the prune cadence and completed/failed TTL and entry caps.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |
    44	
    45	The monitor serializes admissions so append backoff cannot invert a lane. The
    46	default bounded append delays are `0`, `100`, and `300` ms; exhaustion rejects
    47	the transport callback instead of dispatching an event that was not made
    48	durable. At claim time it decodes the versioned payload, re-runs `inspect`, and
    49	rejects an id or lane mismatch before delivery. Set optional `inspectAsync(raw, context)` when inspection needs asynchronous
    50	preparation. Supporting hosts prefer it over `inspect` in both admission and
    51	claim validation. Keep `inspect` as a synchronous fallback for older hosts, which
    52	ignore the companion. Both callbacks must derive the same identity and lane.
    53	The standard raw-event convenience monitor retains its synchronous inspection contract.
    54	
    55	Asynchronous inspection stays inside the existing admission order. Shutdown and `waitForIdle()` join
    56	accepted inspections and their durable appends, including pending claim inspection
    57	when the channel owns its separate delivery grace. Claim inspection rechecks shutdown
    58	and claim cancellation before delivery. Pending claim inspections consume the
    59	existing start slots and keep `onActivityChange` busy until they finish or transfer
    60	to delivery.
    61	
    62	`onDurableAdmission(raw, context)` runs after every durable enqueue, including
    63	duplicates. `context.isNew` is `true` if and only if this admission inserted the
    64	`(queue_name, event_id)` row. It does not indicate claim ownership or eventual
    65	delivery. If retention previously pruned the row, a later admission may insert
    66	it again and report `isNew: true`.
    67	
    68	`deliver` receives `onAdopted`, `onDeferred`, `onAdoptionFinalizing`, `onFailed`,
    69	`onCancelled`, `onAbandoned`, and `abortSignal`. Use `onFailed` for delivery
    70	errors, `onCancelled` for explicit pre-adoption cancellation that must preserve
    71	retry accounting, and `onAbandoned` when a non-adopted turn should consume a
    72	retry attempt. Returning without an explicit handoff marks a terminal
    73	no-dispatch event adopted. `admission` is always `exclusive`. A deferred handoff
    74	keeps the claim held, while shutdown or abort leaves unadopted work retryable.
    75	The monitor tracks delivery independently from claim settlement because
    76	adoption can tombstone a row before the channel's delivery promise returns.
    77	
    78	### One turn, several durable claims
    79	
    80	A channel that answers several inbound events as one turn holds one durable
    81	claim per event, and every one of them has to reach a terminal disposition.
    82	`fanInChannelIngressLifecycles(lifecycles)`, from
    83	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-ingress-runtime`, returns a single
    84	`ChannelIngressLifecycle` that fans each callback out across all of them, plus
    85	`settle`, `abandon`, and `cancel` for the paths that finish outside a callback.
    86	Pass the events' lifecycles in the order they were claimed; `undefined` entries
    87	are skipped, and an empty input yields `lifecycle: undefined` so a caller can
    88	fall back to the single-claim path without branching. Adopting the combined
    89	lifecycle adopts every claim, and abandoning it returns every claim to its retry
    90	budget — no claim is left half-settled. Adoption runs in the order the claims
    91	were passed, and a claim whose adoption is rejected — the drain raises that when
    92	another owner has taken it — fails itself and every claim behind it while the
    93	claims already adopted stay adopted, so a rejection reaches the caller with
    94	nothing left held.
    95	
    96	Use it only when one agent turn genuinely consumes several claims. A channel
    97	that answers each event on its own keeps passing that event's lifecycle
    98	directly.
    99	
   100	### Start slots and deferral
   101	
   102	`drain.startLimit` bounds how many deliveries the drain starts at once. A
   103	delivery that defers normally keeps its slot, because a deferred delivery on the
   104	default `deferredLaneOccupancy: "hold"` still serializes its lane. A drain that
   105	declares `deferredLaneOccupancy: "release"` gives the lane up on deferral, so
   106	its deferred deliveries also give their start slot back, bounded by a budget
   107	equal to `startLimit`: open delivery callbacks stay within `startLimit` plus that
   108	budget, and past it a deferral keeps its slot. Without the release, a handful of
   109	deferred deliveries would hold every slot and stall the other lanes. How much
   110	handed-off deferred work may be pending at once stays the drain owner's
   111	semantics, unchanged by this budget.
   112	
   113	Optional settings include custom append delays, a `drain` option block for
   114	advanced drain ordering/concurrency/retry policy, an external `abortSignal`, a
   115	clock, pump error reporting, a stopped-error factory, and admission policy.
   116	The returned monitor exposes `admit`, `ensureQueueAvailable`, `start`, `pause`,
   117	`stop`, `waitForIdle`, `isRunning`, and `isStopped`. Use the idempotent
   118	`ensureQueueAvailable()` check when plugin-owned migration or preparation must
   119	run after the queue opens but before the drain starts. `stop` first settles
   120	accepted admissions, then aborts and disposes the drain, waits for the pump and
   121	active deliveries, and disposes again to close the lazy-creation race.
   122	
   123	Keep transport-specific redaction, raw-envelope validation, non-retryable
   124	classification, and persisted payload shape in the plugin. Webhook transports
   125	should acknowledge only after `admit` resolves; non-replay transports should
   126	surface durable append exhaustion rather than silently dispatching.
   127	
   128	### Deferred claim heartbeats
   129	
   130	Forward both `onDeferredHeartbeat` and `deferredHeartbeatIntervalMs` when a
   131	plugin wraps the ingress lifecycle or maps it to `turnAdoptionLifecycle`.
   132	`bindIngressLifecycleToReplyOptions(...)` forwards both. The drain derives the
   133	optional cadence from its adoption-stall timeout; fan-in uses the shortest
   134	positive, finite source cadence. The queue renews only while it owns the
   135	lifecycle, stopping after adoption, completion, ownership loss, or callback
   136	failure. A heartbeat does not adopt or complete a claim.
   137	
   138	Wrappers that omit the cadence remain valid but do not enable periodic renewal;
   139	their deferred claims can still reach the adoption watchdog timeout. Plugins
   140	must not run independent timers that keep abandoned work alive.
   141	
   142	## Adapter
   143	
   144	Most plugins define one `message` adapter:
   145	
   146	```ts
   147	import {
   148	  defineChannelMessageAdapter,
   149	  createMessageReceiptFromOutboundResults,
   150	} from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-outbound";
   151	
   152	export const demoMessageAdapter = defineChannelMessageAdapter({
   153	  id: "demo",
   154	  durableFinal: {
   155	    capabilities: {
   156	      text: true,
   157	      replyTo: true,
   158	      thread: true,
   159	      messageSendingHooks: true,
   160	    },
   161	  },
   162	  send: {
   163	    text: async ({ cfg, to, text, accountId, replyToId, threadId, signal }) => {
   164	      const sent = await sendDemoMessage({
   165	        cfg,
   166	        to,
   167	        text,
   168	        accountId: accountId ?? undefined,
   169	        replyToId: replyToId ?? undefined,
   170	        threadId: threadId == null ? undefined : String(threadId),
   171	        signal,
   172	      });
   173	
   174	      return {
   175	        receipt: createMessageReceiptFromOutboundResults({
   176	          results: [{ channel: "demo", messageId: sent.id, conversationId: to }],
   177	          kind: "text",
   178	          threadId: threadId == null ? undefined : String(threadId),
   179	          replyToId: replyToId ?? undefined,
   180	        }),
   181	      };
   182	    },
   183	  },
   184	});
   185	```
   186	
   187	Only declare capabilities the native transport actually preserves. Cover
   188	each declared capability with the matching contract helper exported from this
   189	subpath:
   190	
   191	- send: `verifyChannelMessageAdapterCapabilityProofs(...)`
   192	- durable final delivery: `verifyDurableFinalCapabilityProofs(...)`
   193	- live preview: `verifyChannelMessageLiveCapabilityAdapterProofs(...)` and
   194	  `verifyChannelMessageLiveFinalizerProofs(...)`
   195	- receive ack: `verifyChannelMessageReceiveAckPolicyAdapterProofs(...)`
   196	
   197	## Progress and preview delivery ownership
   198	
   199	Create one `createLivePreviewLifecycle<TPayload, TId>(options)` from
   200	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-outbound` for each admitted reply lifecycle.
   201	It owns final-delivery facts and preview custody. Keep provider operations,
   202	threading, authorization, and native acceptance checks in the channel adapter;
   203	do not keep parallel `finalDelivered` or `previewCommitted` flags.
   204	
   205	The optional `draft` supplies `flush`, `id`,
   206	`discardPending`, `clear`, and, when supported, `seal`. `discardPending` must
   207	stop new updates before awaiting in-flight work. `clear` deletes the captured
   208	provider artifact; returning `false` means deletion was not confirmed.
   209	Native streams without a deletable preview omit `draft` rather than supplying
   210	no-op operations.
   211	
   212	| Option               | Meaning                                                                                                                    |
   213	| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   214	| `retainOnError`      | Keep the preview after an accepted error final. Defaults to `false`; use the channel's existing error presentation policy. |
   215	| `cleanupUndelivered` | Allow cleanup of unused previews when no final was delivered and the turn did not fail. Defaults to `false`.               |
   216	| `onFinalStarted`     | Synchronously stop progress producers when final delivery begins.                                                          |
   217	| `onFinalDelivered`   | Synchronously observe completion of a non-error final. Partial acceptance does not trigger this notification.              |
   218	| `onCleanupFailure`   | Report cleanup failure without replacing an accepted delivery result. The default emits a generic warning.                 |
   219	
   220	Call `deliver({ kind, payload, isError, adapter, deliverNormally, onNormalDelivered })`
   221	at the actual delivery boundary. `deliverNormally` returns a
   222	`LivePreviewDeliveryResult`: the existing channel delivery result with an explicit
   223	`visibleReplySent` boolean and the provider's receipt or message IDs when available.
   224	Do not report queued or locally buffered work as accepted delivery.
   225	
   226	For in-place promotion, `adapter` supplies `buildFinalEdit`, `editFinal`, and any
   227	provider-specific receipt, supplemental-media, or ambiguous-edit handling.
   228	Fresh-final transports omit the edit operations. The owner records promotion
   229	before observers and supplemental delivery, so a later warning cannot edit or
   230	delete the promoted answer.
   231	
   232	`deliver` returns the delivery kind, live-state snapshot, and any accepted
   233	`deliveryResult`. Accepted-partial errors preserve their accepted receipts.
   234	An error from progress flushing is not final-send evidence. Failed or suppressed
   235	final sends do not trigger successful-final cleanup. An explicit supplemental
   236	suppression is not retried; the legacy boolean `false` supplemental result remains
   237	eligible for normal fallback.
   238	
   239	| Operation                              | Use                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
   240	| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   241	| `beginFinalDelivery()`                 | Freeze progress before awaiting provider-owned finalization. This records pending delivery, not acceptance, and does not stop the native transport.                                                                                                                                                   |
   242	| `observeDelivery(result, { isError })` | Record provider-confirmed final delivery through a native or source/message-tool path and retire eligible temporary progress. An invisible result is ignored; a progress receipt is not final evidence. `isError` distinguishes an accepted error response from task success and defaults to `false`. |
   243	| `observeFailure(result?)`              | Record a final dispatcher failure. Pass a provider-confirmed accepted subset for partial delivery; never infer acceptance from the error's class. A previously completed final is not revoked.                                                                                                        |
   244	| `observeSuppression()`                 | Record an intentional final no-send decision, such as cancellation by an outbound modifier hook. It cannot hide an existing delivery failure or revoke accepted content.                                                                                                                              |
   245	| `cleanup({ failed })`                  | Quiesce updates and clean eligible temporary previews. Failed/partial finals and retained or promoted previews stay protected. Cleanup failure cannot authorize resending accepted content.                                                                                                           |
   246	| `retainPreview()`                      | Transfer the artifact out of automatic cleanup, for example after an accepted continuation handoff. This does not claim final delivery.                                                                                                                                                               |
   247	| `reset()`                              | Start the next admitted turn, assistant answer, or block generation. The owner fences stale awaited completions from the new generation. The transport still owns its corresponding message-identity rotation; advance both at the assistant boundary.                                                |
   248	
   249	The read-only `finalStarted`, `finalDelivered`, `finalSucceeded`, `finalFailed`,
   250	`finalSuppressed`, and `previewFinalized` properties are projections of that owner.
   251	`finalDelivered` means some final content was accepted, including partial/error
   252	results; it does not imply completion. `finalSucceeded` requires a complete
   253	non-error final. `finalFailed` identifies failed or partial delivery, not a model
   254	error whose error-message delivery succeeded. Preserve the returned receipt.
   255	`previewFinalized` also covers retained artifacts and accepted replacements
   256	that cannot be promoted again.
   257	
   258	Use the observation operations when provider-owned pagination or deferred
   259	finalization cannot use the generic `deliver` algorithm. Begin before the first
   260	await, then report acceptance, failure, or intentional suppression at actual
   261	settlement. Buffered content and uncertain sends are not visible-final evidence.
   262	
   263	The published `defineFinalizableLivePreviewAdapter` and
   264	`deliverWithFinalizableLivePreviewAdapter` helpers retain their existing
   265	signatures and legacy `void`-means-delivered convention. They call the same
   266	delivery implementation, not a second state machine. New integrations should
   267	use the stateful owner and explicit results. This changes no channel configuration
   268	or streaming default.
   269	
   270	## Outbound echo suppression
   271	
   272	When a platform may redeliver the plugin's own outbound message as inbound, call `recordOutboundMessageIdentity(...)` with the channel, account, conversation, and a stable platform message or source identity. The shared inbound turn path drops matching identities for a bounded 30-second window before session recording or agent dispatch; a source identity may be reserved before send or refreshed when a channel route is removed to close delivery races. `isRecentOutboundMessageIdentity(...)` exposes the same query for channel diagnostics and tests. Do not maintain a parallel channel-local TTL cache for the same stable identity.
   273	
   274	## Plain-text sanitization
   275	
   276	Use `sanitizeForPlainText(...)` when an outbound adapter needs to convert the
   277	supported HTML formatting tags into lightweight text markup. The default keeps
   278	the existing chat-style bold and strikethrough markers. Pass
   279	`{ style: "markdown" }` only when the channel reparses the result as Markdown:
   280	
   281	```ts
   282	import { sanitizeForPlainText } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-outbound";
   283	
   284	const chatText = sanitizeForPlainText(text);
   285	const markdownText = sanitizeForPlainText(text, { style: "markdown" });
   286	```
   287	
   288	The Markdown style uses `**bold**` and `~~strikethrough~~`; italic and inline
   289	code keep `_italic_` and backtick markers in both styles. Select the style at
   290	the channel boundary instead of rewriting marker text after sanitization.
   291	
   292	## Delivery Evidence
   293	
   294	A `MessageReceipt` records the result returned by a channel adapter. Concrete
   295	platform message identifiers show that the platform send path accepted the
   296	message; they do not prove that a recipient's device displayed or read it.
   297	Destination and routing identifiers such as chat, channel, room, conversation,
   298	or recipient JID are metadata, never `platformMessageIds`. Receipts without
   299	platform message identifiers are local receipt metadata only. A
   300	provider-observed receipt thread overrides the requested route thread. If a
   301	batch contains conflicting provider threads, each part retains its thread and
   302	the aggregate receipt omits `threadId`. Channels with read receipts or
   303	device-delivery state should track those facts through a separate
   304	channel-specific path.
   305	
   306	When an adapter intentionally omits a send before dispatch, return
   307	`outcome: "not_sent"` with an empty receipt and no message ID (legacy outbound
   308	adapters use an empty `messageId`). Core records `adapter_returned_no_send` as
   309	an intentional suppression, counts no physical send, and skips send-success
   310	and commit hooks. Do not use this outcome for an acknowledged send without a
   311	platform ID or for an unknown result after dispatch. An empty receipt alone
   312	does not distinguish those states; existing acknowledgement behavior is
   313	unchanged when `outcome` is omitted.
   314	
   315	If a channel adapter can prove that retrying a failure cannot duplicate a
   316	recipient-visible send and no finalization-capable call began, throw
   317	`new PlatformMessageNotDispatchedError("...", { cause: error })` from
   318	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/error-runtime`. Core can then clear stale send-attempt
   319	evidence and safely retry the queued intent. Only the adapter that owns the
   320	final dispatch boundary may make this assertion. Never use the marker after a
   321	finalization/send call begins or returns an ambiguous result; false marking can
   322	duplicate messages.
   323	
   324	## Existing outbound adapters
   325	
   326	If the channel already has a compatible `outbound` adapter, derive the
   327	message adapter instead of duplicating send code:
   328	
   329	```ts
   330	import { createChannelMessageAdapterFromOutbound } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-outbound";
   331	
   332	export const messageAdapter = createChannelMessageAdapterFromOutbound({
   333	  id: "demo",
   334	  outbound,
   335	  durableFinal: {
   336	    capabilities: {
   337	      text: true,
   338	      media: true,
   339	    },
   340	  },
   341	});
   342	```
   343	
   344	Deriving an adapter does not make a channel-owned prepared dispatcher durable.
   345	Route its final sends through the durable helpers while preserving
   346	channel-specific post-send effects and callback-only transport targets.
   347	`message.send.lifecycle.afterSendSuccess` runs after the native send succeeds;
   348	for queued sends, `afterCommit` runs after queue acknowledgment. Keep effects
   349	at the boundary they require rather than leaving them only in a legacy dispatcher.
   350	
   351	## Durable sends
   352	
   353	Runtime send helpers also live on `channel-outbound`:
   354	
   355	- `sendDurableMessageBatch(...)`
   356	- `withDurableMessageSendContext(...)`
   357	- `deliverInboundReplyWithMessageSendContext(...)`
   358	- draft streaming/progress helpers such as `resolveChannelDraftStreamingChunking(...)`
   359	
   360	`sendDurableMessageBatch(...)` and `withDurableMessageSendContext(...)` default
   361	to `durability: "required"`: failure to persist the send intent stops delivery
   362	before the platform call. With `durability: "best_effort"`, a queue-write
   363	failure can fall through to a logged, live-only send without crash recovery.
   364	These durable helpers do not accept `durability: "disabled"`.
   365	
   366	`sendDurableMessageBatch(...)` returns one explicit outcome:
   367	
   368	| Outcome          | Meaning                                                                                 |
   369	| ---------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   370	| `sent`           | at least one visible platform message was accepted by the platform send path            |
   371	| `suppressed`     | no platform message should be treated as missing                                        |
   372	| `partial_failed` | at least one platform message was accepted before a later payload or side effect failed |
   373	| `failed`         | no platform receipt was produced                                                        |
   374	
   375	Use `payloadOutcomes` when a batch mixes sent, suppressed, and failed
   376	payloads. Do not infer hook cancellation from an empty legacy
   377	direct-delivery result.
   378	
   379	A `suppressed` result is ambiguous when its reason is
   380	`adapter_returned_no_identity`, or any payload outcome records a send, an
   381	identityless send, or a failure with `sentBeforeError`. Check the whole batch
   382	before treating suppression as intentional non-delivery. For a known omission,
   383	an action can return `{ status: "suppressed", reason: result.reason }` without
   384	a tool error or a fabricated receipt. Keep free-form hook diagnostics private.
   385	Suppression does not recover an earlier failed send.
   386	
   387	Failure is not permission to send the same payload through another path.
   388	Once admitted, the queue owns retry or reconciliation until its exact owner
   389	acknowledges or terminally retires the intent. Pending custody is not a
   390	delivery receipt: preserve partial receipts, and do not report an unconfirmed
   391	`ask_user` prompt as visible. Gateway `OUTBOUND_DELIVERY_QUEUED` responses mean
   392	delivery is pending and must not be resent; an ambiguous send may need
   393	reconciliation rather than an automatic retry.
   394	
   395	When a transport creates a thread during its first successful send, the
   396	outbound adapter may implement `adoptTargetFromDelivery(...)`. Return the
   397	typed thread ID from the platform receipt and core carries it into later
   398	payloads, pins, and post-delivery hooks in that durable batch. Core never
   399	replaces an explicit caller thread, and it does not infer adoption from
   400	`receipt.threadId` without the adapter opt-in.
   401	
   402	### Automatic unknown-send reconciliation
   403	
   404	Set `message.durableFinal.automaticUnknownSendReconciliation` only when the
   405	plugin can reconcile an ambiguous provider send from persisted, post-policy
   406	state without rerunning modifying hooks or regenerating provider payloads.
   407	Core considers this opt-in after hooks and cancellation, and only for exactly
   408	one accepted prepared payload. Multi-payload batches do not opt in
   409	automatically.
   410	
   411	The adapter must also advertise `capabilities.reconcileUnknownSend: true` and
   412	provide `reconcileUnknownSend(...)`. Use `reconcileUnknownSendKinds` to name
   413	the concrete transport branches the plugin can prove, such as `text` or
   414	`media`. If the kind map is present, the selected branch must be `true`.
   415	Omitting the map means the callback claims every selected branch, so prefer an
   416	explicit map for new plugins.
   417	
   418	The callback must use provider-owned idempotency or authoritative readback to
   419	return `sent` with the actual provider receipt, `not_sent` only when a fresh
   420	send is provably safe, or `unresolved` when neither outcome can be proven.
   421	When reconciliation is explicitly required, unsupported prepared shapes fail
   422	before provider I/O. During recovery, missing, incomplete, or mismatched
   423	provider proof must fail closed rather than replaying content that could
   424	already be visible.
   425	
   426	If reconciliation needs provider-owned persisted evidence, implement
   427	`afterUnknownSendTerminal(...)`. Core calls it after the ambiguous queue row
   428	has authoritatively moved to failed, including retry-budget exhaustion. Use it
   429	to remove provider-owned plans or payloads that are no longer needed. Cleanup
   430	is best effort and must be idempotent; a failure is logged without making the
   431	terminal queue row replayable again.
   432	
   433	## Deferred delivery admission
   434	
   435	Use `message.durableFinal.admitDeferredDelivery(...)` when a resolved account
   436	cannot safely accept core-managed outbound or deferred delivery. Core calls
   437	this hook synchronously before live outbound work, including paths that skip
   438	queue persistence, and again before replaying a recovered intent. The context
   439	includes `cfg`, `channel`, `to`, `accountId`, and a `phase` of `live` or
   440	`recovery`.
   441	
   442	Return `{ status: "allowed" }` to continue. Return
   443	`{ status: "permanent_rejection", reason }` when the delivery must not be
   444	persisted, sent dire
